WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.016 --> 00:00:07.956
Fr&eacute;d&eacute;ric’s fingers flew over the piano keyboard. The sound the six-year-old created with his tiny fingers was heavenly. He played

2
00:00:07.957 --> 00:00:13.195
music he had heard others play and music he had composed himself. Little Fr&eacute;d&eacute;ric loved music so much that

3
00:00:13.196 --> 00:00:18.568
its beauty supposedly made him cry. But as this generally happy boy grew up, he became a melancholy man&#8212;a genius

4
00:00:18.569 --> 00:00:27.209
debilitated by a terminal illness. Despite his troubles, or perhaps because of them, Fr&eacute;d&eacute;ric Chopin created extraordinarily beautiful music

5
00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:35.957
during his short life. Fr&eacute;d&eacute;ric Chopin was born in Zelazowa Wola, near Warsaw, Poland, on March 1, 1810. He was

6
00:00:35.958 --> 00:00:43.537
the second of four children to a French father and a Polish mother. Soon after Fr&eacute;d&eacute;ric was born, the

7
00:00:43.538 --> 00:00:48.256
family moved to Warsaw, where his father worked as a tutor. Music was a constant part of the Chopin household&#8212;his

8
00:00:48.257 --> 00:00:54.756
father played the flute and violin, his mother the piano, and his older sister was a gifted pianist. Young Fr&eacute;d&eacute;ric

9
00:00:54.757 --> 00:01:04.806
taught himself to play by watching his older sister, and by age six, he could play anything he heard.

10
00:01:04.807 --> 00:01:12.567
Before he turned eight, he had already composed two traditional Polish dances and a march. When his first piece of

11
00:01:12.568 --> 00:01:19.170
music was published, a Warsaw newspaper called "Little Chopin" a "musical genius." After his first concert, in February 1818,

12
00:01:19.171 --> 00:01:26.519
he was asked to play at many social gatherings and charity events, and Grand Duke Constantine&#8212;the brother of Czar Alexander

13
00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:42.606
I, the ruler of Poland&#8212;often requested that he play for him. Fr&eacute;d&eacute;ric attended school like other Polish children, but

14
00:01:42.607 --> 00:01:47.765
music was his true love. He began taking private piano lessons when he was six. His teacher introduced him to

15
00:01:47.766 --> 00:01:51.537
the music of Bach and Mozart, whose compositions he would love his entire life. Chopin, however, surpassed his teacher’s abilities

16
00:01:51.538 --> 00:01:58.196
by age 13, and he began lessons with the respected musician Joseph Elsner. In private lessons and later at

17
00:01:58.197 --> 00:02:06.759
Elsner’s Warsaw Conservatory, Elsner had Chopin learn music theory and the techniques of music composition, which strengthened Chopin’s own pieces.

18
00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:19.072
In 1825, Czar Alexander of Russia rewarded the 15-year-old boy with a diamond ring after hearing him play. When

19
00:02:19.073 --> 00:02:27.518
Chopin graduated from the Warsaw Conservatory in 1829, Elsner wrote: "Chopin, Fr&eacute;d&eacute;ric, third-year student; outstanding abilities; musical genius." The young

20
00:02:27.519 --> 00:02:40.221
Chopin had been a popular, fun-loving prankster, and his music was happy and light, but misfortune began to shadow

21
00:02:40.222 --> 00:02:49.158
him in his teens. His always-frail health worsened, and he developed symptoms associated with tuberculosis&#8212;a serious cough and a general

22
00:02:49.159 --> 00:02:57.945
weakness. Strenuous activities, like hiking, were now difficult for him because he couldn’t catch his breath. These symptoms stayed

23
00:02:57.946 --> 00:03:03.723
with him for the rest of his life. When he was 17, his sister died of the same ailment, and

24
00:03:03.724 --> 00:03:06.921
from that time on, a deep melancholy afflicted his life and colored his music. Chopin spent a lot of time

25
00:03:06.922 --> 00:03:15.960
in the countryside of Poland, which his family hoped would improve his health. There, his love for his homeland

26
00:03:15.961 --> 00:03:26.134
only deepened. The songs he heard the peasants sing as they worked in the fields touched him, and Polish folk

27
00:03:26.135 --> 00:03:35.461
music influenced his compositions more strongly than ever before. He had to leave Poland, however, to further his career.

28
00:03:35.462 --> 00:03:47.620
In 1830, while traveling in Europe, he learned of a Polish revolt against Russian rule and of Russia’s brutal suppression

29
00:03:47.621 --> 00:03:54.842
of the rebellion. Chopin was afraid for his family and friends but knew that he could not return and

30
00:03:54.843 --> 00:04:03.874
make a life for himself in Warsaw. Instead, he settled in Paris, where he socialized with other Poles and played

31
00:04:03.875 --> 00:04:13.537
concerts to raise funds for recently arrived Polish immigrants. In Paris, Chopin became a part of high society. He dressed

32
00:04:13.538 --> 00:04:23.124
fashionably, always wearing white gloves, and his manners were perfect. He was a favorite guest at salons&#8212;gatherings of cultured

33
00:04:23.125 --> 00:04:31.651
and wealthy people&#8212;where he would delight his friends by improvising and playing his own works. Unlike other composers, he concentrated

34
00:04:31.652 --> 00:04:36.897
primarily on music for the piano, which was gaining popularity among Paris aristocrats. Chopin wrote innovative music that let

35
00:04:36.898 --> 00:04:44.720
the instrument sing on its own. He made the piano a star, elevating it from its minor role in the

36
00:04:44.721 --> 00:04:54.379
orchestra and an accompanying instrument for soloists. He earned his income by composing new works and teaching the aristocracy,

37
00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:59.173
but intimidated by crowds, he avoided public performances. Over his entire life, he played in public only thirty times. His

38
00:04:59.174 --> 00:05:07.980
interaction with the Parisian upper class allowed Chopin to meet Aurore Dudevant, the woman who would become his partner for

39
00:05:07.981 --> 00:05:16.307
nine years. Dudevant, better known by her pen name, George Sand, was a scandalous, cross-dressing, cigar-smoking novelist. In 1838,

40
00:05:16.308 --> 00:05:24.696
the first year of their relationship, Sand took Chopin to the island of Majorca for a vacation. However, when rumors

41
00:05:24.697 --> 00:05:35.749
that Chopin had tuberculosis surfaced, they were evicted from their hotel. They finished their stay in an old, damp

42
00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:40.456
monastery. Upon their return to Paris, the couple developed a new routine. Chopin taught in Paris from October through May,

43
00:05:40.457 --> 00:05:49.122
and they spent the summers at Sand’s country home&#8212;she wrote, and he composed. It was a happy time, and

44
00:05:49.123 --> 00:05:54.082
yet, Chopin was often depressed. A student reported that he broke chairs and stomped his feet in anger, and Sand

45
00:05:54.083 --> 00:06:06.330
wrote that "no one ever had a temperament so uneven, an imagination more deranged and gloomy." Eventually, Sand tired of

46
00:06:06.331 --> 00:06:14.498
being a mother and nurse to the sickly Chopin, and the relationship began to dissolve. When she wrote a

47
00:06:14.499 --> 00:06:23.482
book in which the male protagonist was whiny, demanding, and dependent, many believed this character was modeled after Chopin. In

48
00:06:23.483 --> 00:06:33.269
1847, the relationship finally ended, marking the end of the composer’s most creative period and the beginning of his

49
00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:40.911
final decline. Life became very difficult for Chopin. His health made teaching and composing a struggle, and he had little

50
00:06:40.912 --> 00:06:53.742
income. He was forced to give several concerts to support himself, after which a friend told him, "You have

51
00:06:53.743 --> 00:07:00.186
gained in suffering and poetry; the melancholy of your compositions penetrates still deeper into one’s heart." In early 1848, a

52
00:07:00.187 --> 00:07:07.282
former student invited Chopin to London and Scotland. There, the many social events he was expected to attend exhausted

53
00:07:07.283 --> 00:07:13.830
him, and his health deteriorated. Spitting blood and coughing so much he could barely breathe, he returned to Paris in

54
00:07:13.831 --> 00:07:19.449
November. Chopin died less than a year later, on October 17, 1849. His body was buried in a Paris cemetery,

55
00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:27.995
but as he had requested in his will, his heart was taken back to the Poland he loved. Fr&eacute;d&eacute;ric

56
00:07:27.996 --> 00:07:36.966
Chopin was a man of contrasts. Born into the Polish middle class, he lived within the highest ranks of Paris

57
00:07:36.967 --> 00:07:42.632
society. He was capable of being delightful and funny but could also be moody and glum. He once said,

58
00:07:42.633 --> 00:07:50.034
"It would be easier for me to reveal my feelings if they could be expressed in musical sounds." And he

59
00:07:50.035 --> 00:08:00.346
did reveal them; all the feelings he experienced throughout his life&#8212;joy, melancholy, and even deep despair&#8212;are heard in his

60
00:08:00.347 --> 00:08:07.214
music. It is perhaps because his music expressed these emotions so well that it still moves people around the world.

